This folder contains fingerprint-based tests for various models.

When a fingerprint test passes, that indicates that with very high
probability, the simulation has followed the same trajectory as when
the fingerprint was recorded, i.e. the times and modules of the events
were the same. That is, a passing fingerprint tests means that the
simulation model logic works the same as before.

However, a passing test does not guarantee that e.g. result recording
has not changed.

Fingerprints are fragile to NED changes and parameter value changes.

When a fingerprint test fails, the simulation's correctness has to be
verified by some other means, and the fingerprints in the tests
updated.

The fingerprints are in store.json. Each entry names one run (working
directory, ini file, config, run number), its sim-time-limit, the
fingerprint ingredients, the expected value and the expected result.
The ethernet-*.ini files of this folder are simulations that exist
only for the fingerprint test.

Run the tests and accept new values with the opp_repl commands, after
the OMNeT++ and INET setenv:

  opp_run_fingerprint_tests --load @opp -p inet [-f <regex>]
  opp_update_fingerprint_test_results --load @opp -p inet [-f <regex>]

doc/src/developers-guide/ch-testing.rst describes the options.

Each concrete config has run-0 fingerprints of tplx, ~tNl and ~tND,
with these exceptions:

- A run whose packets cannot be serialized has no ~tND: ~tND computes
  checksums, CRCs and FCSs, which needs a serializer for each packet.
- showcases/wireless/sensornetwork StatisticLMac expects ERROR: its
  run 0 stops with an error at its limit.
- These configs ask for user input, so they have no fingerprint:
  examples/adhoc/idealwireless Ping2, examples/adhoc/ieee80211 Ping2,
  examples/inet/tcpthroughput Delay-100ms-advWin1MB,
  examples/wireless/lan80211 NHosts (omnetpp-ftp.ini),
  Streaming2 (omnetpp-streaming.ini) and Ping2,
  examples/wireless/lan80211ac General and Ping2,
  tutorials/protocol General of Network90.ini and Network91.ini.

fingerprinttest_selfdoc runs the fingerprint tests with the
self-documentation of INET, to make SelfDoc.json.
